 position  for the capture boats to net them there  then carefully transferred onto a  transport boat first stop a specially  constructed marine hospital for medical  assessment then a short trip to the  vaquita snoo home a large retrofitted  sea pin once used as a tourist aquarium  that was towed here nearly 2,000 miles  from the Pacific the complicated plan  worked at first on day two of the  project they captured one for the first  time ever when you caught the first  juvenile that that first week you must  have felt terrific that you had a chance  to actually succeed oh yeah I mean it  was all Cheers I was just so exciting  that we might actually catch an  individual and maybe  catch enough to save the species but  that six month old female was just too  stressed from the experience they had to  let her go and the team took to the sea  to try again vaquita is in this water  right off of our bow the boats are  moving around right now trying to  encircle him and again and again with  all the species going extinct in this  world every year why is this one worth  saving one every species is worth saving  this is the most endangered Andry mammal  right now so sort of an AA profession  this would be the species that we focus  on but all the effort and expense and  frustration is also to bring the issue  of illegal gill nets to the public's  attention so if we can absolutely ensure  that these underwater gill nets and not  in the place where these remaining  animals are that's they would survive  they could thrive on their own yeah they  just need a chance yeah they just need a  chance for CBS this morning Saturday Don  Dahler Baja Mexico
 

